Paracetamol PRN with partial relief. No recent swimming, flights, or cotton buds deep in canal, but has been “having a bit of a scratch” at canal entrance.  

Background: allergic/atopic-type dermatitis mainly peri-auricular/neck, flexures. On/off hydrocortisone 1% and sorbolene previously. No regular meds currently. NKDA recorded, but sensitive to fragrances. Non-smoker.  

Exam (focused):  
Obs today: BP 118/72, HR 82, afebrile, sats 99% RA. Looks well, comfortable.  
R ear: dry, erythematous, scaly skin behind ear, some excoriations. Mild tenderness with pinna movement but not severe. Canal lateral third a bit red, slightly scaly, no swelling, no debris, no discharge. TM intact, pearly, no bulge/retraction. No mastoid tenderness or swelling.  
L ear normal. Mild nasal congestion only. No cervical nodes.  

Skin (brief): peri-auricular and lateral neck with erythematous, dry, lichenified patches, no obvious secondary infection, no vesicles/pustules.  

Impression:  
- R ear pain – likely referred from peri-auricular dermatitis with mild early otitis externa from scratching. TM normal, no AOM, no mastoiditis. Recent URTI may be contributing a bit but not main issue.  
- Chronic allergic/atopic-type dermatitis (peri-auricular/neck) – mild flare, poor adherence to emollient and steroid. No clear evidence of bacterial or HSV superinfection today.  

Plan:  
Ear / dermatitis  
- Explained likely skin-driven ear pain rather than middle ear infection.  
- Topical: mometasone furoate 0.1% cream OD x 5–7 days very sparingly to outer canal entrance + peri-auricular skin (avoid deeper canal).  
- Restart emollient: sorbolene/paraffin cream BD–QID to affected areas ongoing (script + repeats).  
- For flare elsewhere (face/neck): hydrocortisone 1% thin layer OD up to 5–7 days then stop, continue emollient only.  
- Analgesia: paracetamol 1 g QID PRN (max 4 g/day). Ibuprofen 400 mg TDS PRN with food if needed and no issues.  

Avoid / triggers  
- Asked to stop new shampoo and fragranced products around ears/neck for now; use fragrance-free sensitive products.  
- Asked to bring photos/list of products next visit.  

Adherence  
- Pt acknowledges “I’m hopeless with remembering the creams”. Suggested phone reminders and keeping cream by toothbrush. Gave simple written plan on summary.  
- Scripts with repeats to reduce running out.  

Ix: none today – clinically mild dermatitis/otitis externa, TM normal. If recurrent/persistent, consider swab and derm referral.  

Safety net:  
- RTC/ED if ↑pain, fevers, discharge, hearing loss, spreading redness/swelling esp behind ear, vertigo, severe headache.  

Follow-up:  
- Review 1–2 weeks to check ear pain and skin control (book longer appt if wants full dermatitis work-up/possible derm referral).  

